\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{fig1_pipeline.pdf}
  \caption{Adaptive sample-select-correct pipeline. $K{=}2$ action samples are drawn from the frozen VLA; the argmin-to-centroid sample is selected, and a lightweight CRM applies bounded correction ($\|\boldsymbol{\delta}\|_2 \leq \varepsilon$) only when the pairwise $L_2$ distance exceeds the uncertainty threshold $\tau$.}
  \label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

\looseness=-1 Our method adaptively allocates inference-time compute for a frozen flow-matching VLA (Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline}). At each control step, a $K{=}2$ uncertainty probe determines whether the VLA is confident or uncertain; confident steps output the argmin-to-centroid sample directly, while uncertain steps apply a learned residual correction to the same sample via a lightweight module. The base VLA is never retrained.

\subsection{Problem Setup}
\label{sec:problem_setup}

Let $\pi_\theta$ be a frozen flow-matching VLA that, given an observation $o_t$ (image, language instruction, proprioceptive state), generates action chunks of length $C{=}50$ in $\mathbb{R}^{d \times C}$ with $d{=}6$ (3 translation + 3 rotation). Under receding-horizon execution, only the first $H{=}5$ steps of each chunk influence the robot before re-planning, so we discard the remainder. The gripper dimension is handled separately via direct thresholding and is excluded from the pipeline.

\looseness=-1 Each forward pass of $\pi_\theta$ produces a stochastic action sample; querying independently $K$ times yields $K$ candidate chunks. Our goal is to produce a single action more accurate than any individual sample, with no changes to $\pi_\theta$. The method applies to any flow-matching VLA that produces diverse samples (Appendix~\ref{sec:applicability}).

\subsection{Multi-Sample Selection}
\label{sec:selection}

We draw $K$ action chunks from $\pi_\theta$ via independent flow-matching rollouts and truncate each to $H{=}5$ steps, yielding samples $\{a_h^{(k)}\}_{k=1}^K$ with $a_h^{(k)} \in \mathbb{R}^d$ for horizon step $h = 1, \dots, H$. For each step, we compute the sample mean $\boldsymbol{\mu}_h = \frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^K a_h^{(k)}$ and select the sample with the smallest total $L_2$ distance to the mean across all horizon steps:
\begin{equation}
  a_{\mathrm{base}} = \argmin_{k \in \{1,\dots,K\}} \sum_{h=1}^{H} \bigl\|a_h^{(k)} - \boldsymbol{\mu}_h\bigr\|_2^2.
  \label{eq:argmin}
\end{equation}
Unlike the element-wise mean, which can blend incompatible modes, the argmin returns an actual sample and preserves within-sample temporal coherence.

\subsection{Correction Module (CRM)}
\label{sec:crm}

The CRM is a lightweight MLP that refines the selected action $a_{\mathrm{base}}$ by predicting a bounded residual correction conditioned on the current observation and the selected action itself.

\paragraph{Architecture.}
\looseness=-1 The CRM consists of three fully connected layers with ReLU activations: $\mathrm{Linear}(n_{\mathrm{in}}, 256) \to \mathrm{ReLU} \to \mathrm{Linear}(256, 256) \to \mathrm{ReLU} \to \mathrm{Linear}(256, 30)$, preceded by input normalization. For the primary obs\_only variant ($n_{\mathrm{in}}{=}990$), the input is the concatenation of the frozen observation embedding $\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{obs}} \in \mathbb{R}^{960}$ from SmolVLA's vision-language encoder and the selected action $a_{\mathrm{base}} \in \mathbb{R}^{30}$ ($H{=}5$ steps $\times$ $d{=}6$ DOFs), for a total of 327,198 parameters (${\sim}$327K). The output is a residual $\boldsymbol{\delta} \in \mathbb{R}^{30}$, and the corrected action is:
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{a}^* = a_{\mathrm{base}} + \mathrm{clip}\bigl(\boldsymbol{\delta},\; \|\boldsymbol{\delta}\|_2 \leq \varepsilon\bigr),
  \label{eq:crm}
\end{equation}
where $\varepsilon$ is a norm bound that limits the correction magnitude (Section~\ref{sec:magnitude}). The CRM never sees individual action candidates: it receives only the already-selected $a_{\mathrm{base}}$ and predicts a correction toward the expert action.

\paragraph{Conditioning variants.}
We compare three CRM variants that share the same MLP architecture but differ in their input:
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
  \item \textbf{obs\_only} (primary): $[\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{obs}};\, a_{\mathrm{base}}] \in \mathbb{R}^{990}$, 327K parameters. No conformal or uncertainty features.
  \item \textbf{perdof}: $[\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{obs}};\, a_{\mathrm{base}};\, \mathbf{u}_{\mathrm{dof}}] \in \mathbb{R}^{1030}$, 337K parameters. $\mathbf{u}_{\mathrm{dof}}$ concatenates per-DOF standard deviations, conformal prediction radii~\cite{angelopoulos2023gentle}, and covariance log-volumes~\cite{ledoit2004well} ($40$D total).
  \item \textbf{scalar}: $[\mathbf{z}_{\mathrm{obs}};\, a_{\mathrm{base}};\, u_s] \in \mathbb{R}^{991}$, 327K parameters, where $u_s$ is a single scalar uncertainty summary.
\end{itemize}
